=== Zero-downtime rolling update test ===
Date: 2026-08-17
Method: continuous requests (1/sec, 3s timeout) against
https://taskapp.cognistack.dpdns.org/ while triggering
`kubectl rollout restart deployment/frontend -n taskapp` mid-run.

------------------------------------------------------------
ATTEMPT 1 (no timeout set on curl) — inconclusive
------------------------------------------------------------
A 32-second silent gap appeared with one eventual 504. No timeout on the
curl call meant a hung request just didn't print anything rather than
failing visibly — not useful evidence either way. Re-ran with --max-time 3
to get bounded, visible failures instead of silent gaps.

------------------------------------------------------------
ATTEMPT 2 (--max-time 3, before preStop hook) — REAL GAP FOUND
------------------------------------------------------------
02:36:21 - 200
02:36:23 - 502   <- Traefik couldn't reach the pod being terminated
02:36:27 - 000   <- connection refused entirely
02:36:29 - 200   <- recovered
   ...
02:38:07 - 200
02:38:11 - 000   <- second gap, second pod's termination (2 replicas → 2 swaps)
02:38:13 - 200   <- recovered

RESULT: maxUnavailable:0/maxSurge:1 alone did NOT guarantee zero-downtime.
Root cause: a real, well-documented Kubernetes gap — Service/Ingress
endpoint propagation (kube-proxy/Traefik noticing a pod is gone) lags
slightly behind the pod actually receiving SIGTERM and stopping accepting
connections. Traffic can land on a pod mid-shutdown in that window.

FIX APPLIED: added a `lifecycle.preStop` hook (`sleep 5`) to both
backend and frontend Deployments — delays actual container shutdown after
SIGTERM long enough for the Service/Ingress layer to stop routing to it
first. This is a manifest-level fix, not an application source change.

------------------------------------------------------------
ATTEMPT 3 (--max-time 3, after preStop hook) — CLEAN
------------------------------------------------------------
90/90 requests returned 200. Zero failures, zero gaps, across the full
90-second window including the rollout trigger. Full raw log:
(see zero-downtime-raw-3.log in this same directory for the complete,
unedited 90-line output)

------------------------------------------------------------
NOTE ON HOW THIS WAS FOUND
------------------------------------------------------------
The operator independently noticed the 502s consistently coincided with
running the rollout command in a second terminal, before being told the
diagnosis — an observation that matched the timestamp analysis exactly
and confirmed the failures were a real, reproducible rollout-timing issue
rather than incidental network noise.